<DocsLayout
  slug="systems-of-record"
  title="ML Systems of Record | MIAR Documentation"
  description="How Git, DVC, Cloudflare R2, MLflow, MIAR's model registry, and runtime state divide MLOps responsibilities."
>
  <section id="principle">
    <h2>One question, one authoritative answer</h2>
    <p>
      MIAR does not ask one tool to solve every MLOps problem. Each system owns a bounded record,
      while stable identifiers connect the corpus, run, package, approval, deployment, and resulting
      detections.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="records">
    <h2>Authoritative systems</h2>
    <div class="docs-table-wrap">
      <table class="docs-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>System of record</th><th>Authoritative object</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>What code and data pointer defined the build?</td><td>Git</td><td>Commit and reviewed configuration</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Which exact corpus was used?</td><td>DVC</td><td>Content-addressed corpus revision</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Where are large binaries stored?</td><td>Cloudflare R2</td><td>Object checksum and immutable object key</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What happened during training and evaluation?</td><td>MLflow</td><td>Experiment run, parameters, metrics, and artifacts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Is the model approved and deployable?</td><td>MIAR model registry</td><td>Package version, policy result, approval, and rollback state</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What is serving predictions now?</td><td>MIAR runtime</td><td>Loaded package checksum and health state</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="flow">
    <h2>Release flow</h2>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Resolve</strong><p>Git and DVC reconstruct the intended code, configuration, labels, and corpus.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>02</span><div><strong>Observe</strong><p>MLflow records the run identity, parameters, metrics, benchmark evidence, and produced artifacts.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Package</strong><p>The release job assembles weights and evidence into a checksummed, immutable bundle in R2.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>04</span><div><strong>Promote</strong><p>The MIAR registry evaluates policy gates and records approval, rejection, or supersession.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>05</span><div><strong>Serve</strong><p>The runtime loads an approved checksum and attaches package identity to every inference result.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="mlflow">
    <h2>What MLflow is, and what it is not</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/ml/tracking/" rel="external">MLflow Tracking</a>
      makes experiments inspectable. It records parameters, metrics, tags, and artifacts for a run,
      which lets engineers compare training attempts and explain a benchmark result.
    </p>
    <p>
      MLflow is not MIAR's approval authority and is not the durable home for the governed training
      corpus. A run can exist in MLflow without being safe to deploy. Promotion happens only after
      the package and policy contract pass in the MIAR registry.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-callout">
      <span>Operational boundary</span>
      <strong>MLflow says what happened. MIAR decides what may run.</strong>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="storage">
    <h2>Why datasets and model releases use separate R2 prefixes</h2>
    <p>
      DVC stores dataset objects by content identity and manages their transfer. Released model
      bundles use named, immutable package paths so operators can inspect, verify, promote, and
      roll back a human-readable release without navigating DVC's cache layout. Both remain in
      object storage, but their lifecycle contracts are different.
    </p>
  </section>

